{red|**Reflect on your thoughts and feelings about dating again.**

How do you feel about the prospect of dating?}{red| Excited?}{red| Anxious?}{red| Ambivalent?}{red| What concerns do you have about dating after divorce?}{red| What do you hope to find through dating?}{red| What feels different about dating now versus before you were married?}

Write about your dating thoughts:

{red|**Assess your readiness for dating.**

**Review the signs of readiness and unreadiness.** Which describe you?}{red| Have you done sufficient healing work to date healthily?}{red| Are you dating or wanting to date from a healthy place or from desperation?}{red| What would need to be true for you to be fully ready?}

Write your readiness assessment:

{red|**If you have children, their wellbeing factors into your dating readiness.**

How are your children adjusting to the divorce?}{red| Are they stable enough for you to introduce dating?}

{red|Will your dating take time and energy from children who need you?}

{red|Are you prepared to navigate the complications of introducing romantic interests to your children?}

**Your children's needs do not mean you can never date.** But they should be considered in your timing and approach.

{red|Even if you are ready to date, how do you actually meet people?}

**This practical question stumps many divorced adults.** You are no longer in school or the social scenes of young adulthood. Your life is established in ways that may not naturally introduce you to potential partners.

**Multiple methods exist, each with advantages and drawbacks.** Understanding your options helps you choose approaches that fit your situation and values.

{red|**Reflect on how you might meet potential partners.**

What methods appeal to you?}{red| Which do not?}{red| What are your concerns about online dating specifically?}{red| What opportunities exist in your current life to meet people?}{red| What would you need to do to expand your opportunities?}

Write about your meeting method thoughts:

{red|**Practical boundaries protect your time, resources, and life.**

How much time will you give to dating versus other responsibilities?}

{red|How soon will you introduce someone to your children?}

{red|How much will you share about your divorce early on?}

{red|Will you date only Christians or are you open to others?}

{red|How will you handle financial aspects of dating?}

**These practical boundaries vary by person but thinking through them in advance helps you act wisely.**

{red|**Reflect on boundaries in your dating life.**

Where have you struggled with boundaries in dating, past or present?}{red| What emotional boundaries do you need?}{red| What physical boundaries are you committed to?}{red| What practical boundaries would serve you well?}

Write your boundary reflection:

{red|**Reflect on sharing your divorce story.**

How do you feel about sharing your divorce with people you date?}{red| What parts of your story feel most vulnerable to share?}{red| How have past disclosure experiences gone?}{red| What would wise sharing look like for you?}

Write your story reflection:

{red|**When you are a parent, you must evaluate potential partners not just as partners but as potential stepparents.**

How do they relate to children in general?}

{red|How do they treat your children when they meet?}

{red|Do they understand and respect your parenting responsibilities?}

{red|Are they patient with the complications children bring?}

{red|Do they have realistic expectations about blended family dynamics?}

**Someone wonderful for you might not be right for your children.** Both matter.

{red|**Reflect on how children factor into your dating.**

How are your children adjusting to the divorce?}{red| What concerns do you have about introducing dating to them?}{red| How will you balance your dating with their needs?}{red| What qualities must a partner have in relation to your children?}

Write about your children and dating:

{red|**Reflect on which pitfalls you are most susceptible to.**

Which of these pitfalls are you most likely to fall into?}{red| Have you already made any of these mistakes?}{red| What would help you avoid these pitfalls going forward?}{red| What would trusted friends say about your dating tendencies?}

Write your pitfall awareness:

{red|**Reflect on how faith integrates with your dating.**

How does your faith shape who you are willing to date?}{red| How does it shape how you date?}{red| Where do you struggle to honor God in dating?}{red| What would it look like to fully integrate your faith into your dating life?}

Write about faith and your dating:

{red|**Reflect on how you handle rejection and disappointment.**

How have you handled dating rejection in the past?}{red| Do you tend to take rejection too personally?}{red| How does your divorce experience affect how you handle rejection?}{red| What would help you handle disappointment more healthily?}

Write about your rejection experience:

{red|What is the most important thing you have learned about dating through this journey?}

{red|What do you want to remember as you navigate dating after divorce?}

Write your key dating insight:

{red|What commitments will you make about dating?}

{red|How will you approach dating with wisdom rather than desperation?}{red| How will you maintain your boundaries and values?}{red| How will you handle the challenges of dating after divorce?}{red| How will you honor God in your dating life?}

